Overlay patch 0060: THE OCARINA NOTE TIMER MUST NOT WRAP (GitHub issue #1, part 1).

SYMPTOM (1.1.1, iPhone). Recording Bonooru's scarecrow song, or playing it back
to summon Pierre, ended about nine times in ten with the ocarina staff still on
screen and nothing happening; only a scene reload or a relaunch got out of it.
Reported with a root cause, a host simulation and an on-device test by GMoxFr.

CAUSE. Audio_OcaPlayback (soh/src/code/code_800EC960.c) counts note lengths in
audio TASKS (gAudioContext.totalTaskCnt, sampled once per game frame). When a
frame overshoots the current note, the excess is carried into the next note
with an unsigned subtraction:

    sNotePlaybackTimer -= nextNoteTimerStep;

That is only safe while the overshoot is shorter than the next note. On console
and in every SoH release audio runs in lock-step with the game: exactly three
tasks per frame, and recorded notes are at least three tasks long, so the timer
cannot wrap. The pinned upstream (417256b4) carries HarbourMasters/Shipwright
#6704, the self-pumping audio thread, which wakes every 5 ms: a game frame now
sees 0, 3, 6 or 9+ tasks depending on when CoreAudio pulls its buffers. A
scarecrow song is recorded from the player's own timing and is full of 3-task
entries (short notes, short rests, one entry per pitch-bend wobble of the
touch stick). A 6-task frame landing on a 3-task entry wraps the timer to
~4e9; playback never finishes and MSGMODE_DISPLAY_SONG_PLAYED /
MSGMODE_SCARECROW_PLAYBACK wait forever. The built-in songs have long notes,
which is why only the scarecrow song locks.

The reporter's host simulation of the recorder plus Audio_OcaPlayback against
the iOS audio cadence: lock-step 0 % softlocks; self-pump 19.6 % (clean song)
and 90.5 % (song with stick wobble); self-pump plus this fix 0 % and 0 %.
Upstream reverted #6704 on 2026-10-01 (#7284); its replacement (#7289) still
lets several batches run per frame, so this fix does not depend on how the
audio thread is paced.

FIX. __IOS__ only. If the overshoot reaches the new note's length, the note
ends on the next update instead of wrapping the timer. Lock-step pacing never
takes the new branch, so vanilla timing is unchanged. On device (reporter,
iPhone 18 Pro Max, iOS 27.2): ten plays in a row, no softlock; the unpatched
1.1.1 still locks.

--- a/soh/src/code/code_800EC960.c
+++ b/soh/src/code/code_800EC960.c
@@ -1789,7 +1789,24 @@
                 }
                 return;
             } else {
+#ifdef __IOS__
+                // SOH_IOS (overlay 0060): never wrap the u32 note timer. With
+                // the self-pumping audio thread (upstream #6704) one game frame
+                // can see 6-9+ audio tasks instead of exactly R_UPDATE_RATE, so
+                // the overshoot carried into this note can exceed its length --
+                // and a player-recorded scarecrow song is full of 3-task
+                // entries. The subtraction then wraps to ~4e9 tasks,
+                // sPlaybackState never reaches 0, and the textbox waits on the
+                // staff forever. An overshot note ends on the next update
+                // instead. Lock-step pacing never takes the new branch.
+                if (nextNoteTimerStep < sNotePlaybackTimer) {
+                    sNotePlaybackTimer -= nextNoteTimerStep;
+                } else {
+                    sNotePlaybackTimer = 0;
+                }
+#else
                 sNotePlaybackTimer -= nextNoteTimerStep;
+#endif
             }
 
             if (sNotePlaybackVolume != sPlaybackSong[sPlaybackNotePos].volume) {
